Scenario 1: Disk is 100% Full

Problem:

Your application suddenly stops writing logs.
No space left on device

============================================================

Step 1:
Check disk usage

df -h

Output

Filesystem      Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/sda1        50G 50G     0 100% /

Root filesystem is full.

------------------------------------------------------

Step 2

Find which directory is consuming space.

du -sh /*

or

du -sh /var/*

Output

40G /var/log

========================================================

Step 3

Find large files.

find /var/log -type f -size +500M

Output

/var/log/app.log


=======================================================

Step 4

Rotate or remove logs.

truncate -s 0 /var/log/app.log

or

rm old.log


Why use truncate instead of rm?

Answer:

Because deleting an open log file doesn't immediately free disk space if a process still has it open. truncate clears the contents while keeping the file descriptor valid.

